#: ../../Device Manager.rst:75
msgid "Device Manager Website"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:77
msgid ""
"The device manager comes with a navigation menu on the left hand side of "
"the screen. Otherwise, regardless of what page you are on, there are a "
"few helpful buttons you should know about."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:81
msgid "Connect / Device name"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:83
msgid ""
"The bottom center of the screen is where you connect to your device and "
"see which you are connected to, as well as other info such as the site "
"version and your device's CCOS version."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:86
msgid "Undo and Redo"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:-1
msgid "The Undo and Redo arrows"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:92
msgid ""
"Near the top left corner, the device manager has handy undo and redo "
"buttons which do exactly what their names describe. If you're making "
"changes to your layout, your chords, or your layout, you can step back, "
"one change at a time, all the way back to the very first change that you "
"made during that session. Once you're stepped back, you can step forward "
"to redo the change(s) that was/were undone."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:95
msgid "Color Scheme"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:96
msgid ""
"On the bottom right of the device manager, you'll see a circle with a "
"solid color. Hovering over this circle will reveal the label \"color "
"scheme.\" You can click this circle to change the color scheme of the "
"device manager. In the color scheme menu, you can choose your preferred "
"color using a color pallette, an RGB color system, or by clicking the "
"dropper icon to choose a color on your screen."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:-1
msgid "The Color Scheme Menu"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:103
msgid "Light and Dark Mode"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:104
msgid ""
"Also in the bottom right-hand corner, you'll find a sun or moon icon "
"where you can toggle between light and dark mode. This toggle can help "
"those who would rather a brighter screen to see better or a darker screen"
" to reduce eye strain."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:107
msgid "Save Button"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:-1
msgid "The Save Button"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:113
msgid ""
"If you make changes in the :ref:`library<Device Manager:Library>`, the "
":ref:`layout editor<Device Manager:Layout>` or the :ref:`device "
"menu<Device Manager:Settings Menu>`, a colored \"save\" button will pop "
"up on your screen, towards the top left corner."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:116
msgid "Your changes will not take effect until you click the save button."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:120
msgid "Device"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:121
msgid ""
"The Device Tab is the place where you can configure most settings of your"
" :ref:`connected<Device Manager:Connecting to the Device Manager>` CCOS "
"device and create backups. Read on to see the different settings you can "
"change. You can find more detailed explanations in the "
":doc:`GTM<GenerativeTextMenu>` section."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:125
msgid "Backup Section"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:-1
msgid "The Backup Menu"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:131
msgid ""
"The Backup Menu is home to your backups as well as the place to go to in "
"order to restore your device by using a backup file. There are different "
"kinds of backups that you can create and we'll cover all of them in the "
":ref:`backups<Device Manager:Backups>` section."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:133
msgid ""
"If you toggle the \"Auto-backup\" on, then the website will store a copy "
"of your backup on your browser.  The backup is stored in the browser that"
" you're using at that time and remains on your computer, so only YOU can "
"access it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:135
msgid ""
"On the Device Manager, you can create backups of your chords, your "
"layout, and even your settings. Follow the steps below to create a backup"
" and to restore saved backups to your :doc:`CCOS<CCOS>` device."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:138
msgid "Creating a Backup"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:140 Manager.rst:158 Manager.rst:435 Manager.rst:468
#: Manager.rst:489 Manager.rst:565
msgid ""
"In order to follow these steps, you must already have your device "
":ref:`connected<Device Manager:Connecting to the Device Manager>` to the "
"device manager."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:142 Manager.rst:160
msgid "Open the Device tab and locate the \"Backup\" menu in the top left."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:144
msgid ""
"Choose an \"individual backup\" to download to your computer, or select "
"\"download everything\" to download a single file with all three parts. "
"The file(s) will be downloaded in .json format."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:147
msgid ""
"You can make individual backups of just your chords, just your layout, or"
" just your settings. The \"download everything\" option will download all"
" three of these in a single file instead of three separate files."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:149
msgid ""
"If prompted, select a location to save to on your computer and rename the"
" file to your liking."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:151
msgid "Congratulations! Now you have created a backup."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:154
msgid "Restoring from a Backup"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:155
msgid ""
"Additionally, you can restore your chords, your layout, and your settings"
" on the Device Manager. Follow the steps below to do so."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:162
msgid "Click on \"Restore\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:164
msgid "Select a file to use to restore from. This file should be in .json format."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:167
msgid ""
"Files that you can restore from will have been created ahead of time by "
"following the :ref:`steps to create a backup<Device Manager:Creating a "
"Backup>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:169
msgid ""
"If there are changes, the :ref:`save button<Device Manager:Save Button>` "
"will appear on the top left. Note the changes in the appropriate tab. If "
"you restored chords, check the :ref:`chords tab<Device Manager:Chord "
"Manager>`, if you restored a layout, check the :ref:`layout tab<Device "
"Manager:Layout>`, and if you restored settings, check the :ref:`settings "
"tab<Device Manager:Settings Menu>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:172
msgid ""
"The restore feature does NOT erase data from your device. If there is a "
"conflict (such as a changed setting, a different key in the layout, or a "
"chord that has a different :ref:`output<Chords:Chord Output>`), that will"
" be overwritten by the restore file. Settings and layout backups ALWAYS "
"overwrite everything."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:174
msgid ""
"Once you see the changes that the restore file made, you can click "
":ref:`save<Device Manager:Save Button>` to apply the changes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:177
msgid "Device Section"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:181
msgid ""
"Here you'll find a helpful toggle labeled \"Auto-connect\". By enabling "
"this, the device manager will automatically connect your paired device "
"through a :doc:`serial connection<SerialAPI>` every time that you open "
"it. In doing so, it will also read your chords to detect changes that you"
" may have made since the last time you connected it. If you have this "
"enabled, you won't have to manually connect your device to the manager "
"ever again!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:188
msgid ""
"The :ref:`boot message<GenerativeTextMenu:Startup>` and :ref:`realtime "
"feedback<GenerativeTextMenu:Realtime Feedback>` can be enabled or "
"disabled in this box. Additionally, you can reset some parts of your "
"device files such as your chords, your layout, and even return to factory"
" settings."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:-1
msgid "The Device settings box"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:196
msgid "Spurring"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst
msgid "What is Spurring?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:200
msgid ""
"Spurring is a ‘chording only’ mode which tells your device to output "
":ref:`chords<Chords:What are Chords?>` on a press event rather than a "
"press and release event. When in spurring mode, you can press the keys of"
" a :ref:`chord<Chords:What are Chords?>` one at a time with a much longer"
" waiting period, which makes it a useful mode for those who want to "
"practice chording without worrying about proper "
":ref:`timing<GenerativeTextMenu:Press Tolerance>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:202
msgid ""
"Spurring mode also enables you to jump from one :ref:`chord<Chords:What "
"are Chords?>` to another without releasing everything. It can provide "
"significant speed gains when chording, but also takes away the "
"flexibility of character entry. Spurring mode can truly maximize speed "
"when chording if a user has chords for all of the words they want to use."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:-1
msgid "The Spurring settings box"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:208
msgid ""
"In this box, you can enable or disable spurring mode as well as increase "
"or decrease the :ref:`spurring timeout "
"setting<GenerativeTextMenu:Spurring Timeout>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:211
msgid "Arpeggiates"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst
msgid "What are arpeggiates?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:214
msgid ""
"Arpeggiate actions are timed actions that can modify a "
":ref:`chord<Chords:What are Chords?>` after the chord is performed. A "
"quick example of this is the use of :ref:`chord modifiers<Device "
"Manager:Chord Modifiers>` after you perform the chord. You can read that "
"section for information on how the chord modifiers work."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:216
msgid ""
"With arpeggiates enabled, you can chord the word run and then, within the"
" :ref:`arpeggiate timeout window<GenerativeTextMenu:Arpeggiate Timeout>`,"
" press the :ref:`past tense modifier<Device Manager:Past Tense>` for the "
"word to be “modified” into its past tense variant; in english, ran."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:-1
msgid "The Arpeggiates settings box"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:222
msgid ""
"In this box, ou can enable or disable arpeggiates as well as increase or "
"decrease the :ref:`arpeggiate timeout "
"setting<GenerativeTextMenu:Arpeggiate Timeout>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:225
msgid "Chord Modifiers"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst
msgid "What are chord modifiers?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:228
msgid ""
"Chord modifiers are actions that change a chord when "
":ref:`chorded<Chords:What are Chords?>` at the same time as the "
":ref:`chord input<Chords:Chord Input>`, or when pressed immediately after"
" (arpeggiately) the :ref:`chord<Chords:What are Chords?>`, provided that "
":ref:`arpeggiates<GenerativeTextMenu:Arpeggiate>` are enabled."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:230
msgid ""
"As of February of 2024, only the CharaChorder One and CharaChorder Lite "
"support the use of chord modifiers. Additionally, as of that same time, "
"chord modifiers only work in English."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:233
msgid ""
"Chord modifiers are NOT the same as keyboard modifiers. Keyboard "
"modifiers affect keys pressed on a keyboard. Those keys include ``CTRL``,"
" ``ALT``, and ``FN``. Chord modifiers affect chords."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:-1
msgid "The Chord Modifiers settings box"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:239
msgid ""
"In this box, you can read a brief explanation of chord modifiers and how "
"to access them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:242
msgid "Capitalization"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:243
msgid ""
"The capitalization modifier modifies any chord so that the first letter "
"is capitalized on :ref:`output<Chords:Chord Output>`. This "
":ref:`modifier<Device Manager:Chord Modifiers>` can be performed together"
" with a :ref:`chord<Chords:What are Chords?>` or "
":ref:`arpeggiately<GenerativeTextMenu:Arpeggiate>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:245
msgid ""
"The capitalization modifier is located on the ``SHIFT`` key. In the "
":ref:`layout editor<Device Manager:Layout>`, this key can be either "
"\"Shift Keyboard Modifier (Left)\" or \"Shift Keyboard Modifier "
"(Right)\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:248
msgid ""
"If you have ``CAPS LOCK`` active, all letters in a chord will be "
"capitalized except the first one when using this modifier."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:251
msgid "Present Tense"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:252
msgid ""
"The present tense modifier modifies supported chords so that they turn "
"into their present tense variants. The word \"run\" would be modified "
"into \"running\" and the word \"work\" would be turned into \"working\". "
"This :ref:`modifier<Device Manager:Chord Modifiers>` can be performed "
"together with a :ref:`chord<Chords:What are Chords?>` or "
":ref:`arpeggiately<GenerativeTextMenu:Arpeggiate>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:254
msgid ""
"The present tense modifier has different locations depending on your "
"device. On the CharaChorder One, this modifier is linked to the "
"\"AMBIDEXTROUS THROWOVER (LEFT)\" key. On the CharaChorder Lite, it's "
"linked to the \"NUMERIC LAYER (LEFT)\" key."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:257
msgid "Pluralizer"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:258
msgid ""
"The pluralizer modifier makes supported chords plural. It will add an "
"\"s\" or \"es\" to the end of supported chords. \"Box\" will turn into "
"\"boxes\" and \"dog\" will become \"dogs\". This :ref:`modifier<Device "
"Manager:Chord Modifiers>` can be performed together with a "
":ref:`chord<Chords:What are Chords?>` or "
":ref:`arpeggiately<GenerativeTextMenu:Arpeggiate>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:260
msgid ""
"The pluralizer modifier has different locations depending on your device."
" On the CharaChorder One, it's linked to the \"AMBIDEXTROUS THROWOVER "
"(RIGHT)\" key. On the CharaChorder Lite, it's linked to the \"RIGHT "
"SPACEBAR\" key, not to be confused with the \"SPACE\" key."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:263
msgid "Past Tense"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:264
msgid ""
"The past tense modifier modifies supported chords so that they turn into "
"their past tense variants. The word \"run\" would be modified into "
"\"ran\". The word \"work\" would be turned into \"worked\". This "
":ref:`modifier<Device Manager:Chord Modifiers>` can be performed together"
" with a :ref:`chord<Chords:What are Chords?>` or "
":ref:`arpeggiately<GenerativeTextMenu:Arpeggiate>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:266
msgid ""
"The past tense modifier has different locations depending on your device."
" On the CharaChorder One, it's linked to the \"NUMERIC LAYER (LEFT)\" "
"key. On the CharaChorder Lite, it's linked to the \"SPACE\" key, not to "
"be confused with the \"RIGHT SPACEBAR\" key."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:269
msgid "Comparative"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:270
msgid ""
"The comparative modifier modifies supported chords so that they turn into"
" their comparative variant. \"Big\" becomes \"bigger\" and \"small\" "
"turns into \"smaller\". This :ref:`modifier<Device Manager:Chord "
"Modifiers>` can be performed together with a :ref:`chord<Chords:What are "
"Chords?>` or :ref:`arpeggiately<GenerativeTextMenu:Arpeggiate>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:272
msgid ""
"The comparative modifier is located on the \"NUMERIC LAYER (RIGHT)\" key "
"on both the CharaChorder One and the CharaChorder Lite."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:276
msgid "Character Entry"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst
msgid "What is Character Entry?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:279
msgid ""
"Character entry, known to the CharaChorder community as \"chentry,\" "
"refers to typing one character at time."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:-1
msgid "The Character Entry settings box"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:285
msgid ""
"In this box, you can change a few settings that relate to using your "
"device for character entry."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst
msgid "Swap Keymap 0 and 1"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:289
msgid ""
"This setting will swap the behavior of the two keys on the bottom-left of"
" the CharaChorder Lite."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:291
msgid ""
"Traditional QWERTY keyboards keep the ``CTRL`` key at the bottom left "
"corner of the keyboard with the ``GUI`` key (Command key on Mac, Windows "
"key on Windows, Super key on Linux, etc.) to the right of the ``CTRL`` "
"key. The CharaChorder Lite has these two keys swapped by default, which "
"some users find odd and difficult to adjust to. A brand new CharaChorder "
"Lite will have the ``GUI`` key at the bottom-left corner with the "
"``CTRL`` key to the right of the ``GUI`` key."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:293
msgid ""
"With this setting, you can effectively swap the two keys’ location at the"
" level of the CCOS so that CTRL is at the bottom-left corner."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst
msgid "Character Entry (chentry)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:297
msgid ""
"This setting is a toggle that disables chording capabilities on CCOS "
"devices. It is off by default and can be enabled in case we don’t want "
"any chording at all. This setting can be useful in cases where we don’t "
"want to accidentally trigger chords unintentionally, such as when gaming."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:299
msgid ""
"If your CCOS device suddenly loses its chording ability, it’s a good idea"
" to check if this setting is toggled off."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst
msgid "Key Scan Rate"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:303
msgid ""
"The scan rate, sometimes known as the “Key scan duration,” refers to the "
"frequency at which the device checks the state of the input keys. For "
"reference, 5 ms corresponds to 200 Hz, which means that :doc:`CCOS<CCOS>`"
" checks the position of the keys once every 5 milliseconds, which equals "
"200 times in a second. Having a lower number is usually better as it "
"makes CCOS more responsive, though the difference at low numbers is "
"usually negligible. In the GTM, this setting is adjustable in millisecond"
" (ms) units."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst
msgid "Key Debounce Press"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:307
msgid ""
"The debounce press setting refers to the time frame (measured in "
"milliseconds) in which :doc:`CCOS<CCOS>` will filter out duplicate key "
"activations on a press event. In other words, any duplicate activations "
"within the given time frame will only be counted as one."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:309
msgid ""
"We should adjust this setting if we are having unintentional duplicate "
"characters while typing. Increasing this value will lower the probability"
" that unwanted duplicate characters will appear because it tells "
":doc:`CCOS<CCOS>` to wait longer before typing an additional character "
"that’s assigned to the same switch-direction. However, having this "
"setting set too high might also cause issues with :doc:`CCOS<CCOS>` not "
"reading intentional double-presses, so it’s recommended to try different "
"numbers in small increments. This setting should be used in connection "
"with the debounce release setting."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst
msgid "Key Debounce Release"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:313
msgid ""
"The debounce release setting refers to the time frame (measured in "
"milliseconds) in which :doc:`CCOS<CCOS>` will filter out duplicate key "
"activations on a release event. In other words, any duplicate activations"
" within the given time frame will only be counted as one."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:315
msgid ""
"We should adjust this setting if we are having unintentional duplicate "
"characters while typing. Increasing this value will lower the probability"
" that unwanted duplicate characters will appear because it tells "
":doc:`CCOS<CCOS>` to wait longer before typing an additional character "
"that’s assigned to the same switch-direction. However, having this "
"setting set too high might also cause issues with :doc:`CCOS<CCOS>` not "
"reading intentional double-presses, so it’s recommended to try different "
"numbers in small increments. This setting should be used in connection "
"with the debounce press setting."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst
msgid "Output Character Delay"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:319
msgid ""
"This setting adds a small delay to keystroke inputs. It is measured in "
"microseconds (μs) and is very small by default."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:321
msgid ""
"You should increase this value if your computer is not accepting all of "
"the characters output by your device, such as when using the "
":doc:`GTM<GenerativeTextMenu>`. If you are having this issue, your "
":doc:`GTM<GenerativeTextMenu>` would look weird, with missing chunks or "
"characters."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:323
msgid ""
"If you have a faster computer, then you can lower this setting to make "
"chording and the :doc:`GTM<GenerativeTextMenu>` feel snappier and more "
"responsive."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:326
msgid "Mouse"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst
msgid "Mouse???"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:329
msgid ""
":doc:`CCOS<CCOS>` has mouse functionality. This means that your "
"CharaChorder, or CCOS-powered keyboard, has the ability to control your "
"computer's mouse. These settings affect the mouse usage on your "
"CharaChorder."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:-1
msgid "The Mouse settings box"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:335
msgid ""
"In this box, you can adjust settings relating to :doc:`CCOS'<CCOS>` mouse"
" abilities."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst
msgid "Mouse Speed(s)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:339
msgid ""
":doc:`CCOS<CCOS>` has two mouse speeds, a fast speed and a slow speed. "
"The slow speed is activated when you use only one of the mouse keys in a "
"single direction (as opposed to using 2 keys in the same direction). The "
"fast speed is activated when you use two mouse keys in a single direction"
" (as opposed to using only one key in the same direction)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:341
msgid ""
"You can read a more in-depth explanation of mouse speeds in the :ref:`GTM"
" section<GenerativeTextMenu:Slow Speed>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst
msgid "Scroll Speed"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:345
msgid ""
"Scroll speed refers to the speed at which your :doc:`CCOS<CCOS>` scroll "
"will scroll."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:347
msgid ""
"You can read a more in-depth explanation of the scroll speed in the "
":ref:`GTM section<GenerativeTextMenu:Scroll Speed>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst
msgid "Active Mouse"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:351
msgid ""
"Active mode nudges your mouse cursor one pixel every minute or so (not a "
"specific timing). This setting can be used to keep your computer from "
"going to sleep. You might turn this setting off if you notice desktop "
"apps are preventing you from getting mobile notifications (for example on"
" Discord or Microsoft Teams)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst
msgid "Poll Rate"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:355
msgid ""
"The polling rate (poll rate) is the frequency at which data from the "
"CharaChorder’s mouse functionality is sent to the device it’s connected "
"to. In other words, how often it updates the cursor’s position to the "
"computer."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:357
msgid ""
"You can read a more in-depth explanation of the polling rate in the "
":ref:`GTM section<GenerativeTextMenu:Poll Rate>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:360
msgid "Chording"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst
msgid "What is Chording?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:363
msgid ""
"Chording is the beautiful ability of pressing multiple keys at a time to "
"get a predefined output, whether it's a single word, an entire phrase, or"
" important addresses."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:365
msgid ""
"A chord is a type of input/output action on a keyboard: you press two or "
"more keys at the same time and release them at the same time, after which"
" a predefined output will replace the originally pressed keys."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:367
msgid ""
"By chording, we are able to type one word at a time instead of one letter"
" at a time. It’s even possible to have chords for phrases and entire "
"sentences."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:-1
msgid "The Chording settings box"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:373
msgid ""
"In this box, you can adjust settings relating to :doc:`CCOS'<CCOS>` "
":doc:`chording<Chords>` abilities as well as turn off "
":doc:`chording<Chords>` alltogether, should you choose to."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst
msgid "Auto-delete Timeout"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:377
msgid ""
"This setting will change how long CCOS counts time in order to replace "
"characters that precede a chord."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:379
msgid ""
"CCOS devices have a running timer that starts after every single "
"character that is entered in traditional chentry (character entry, i.e. "
"one letter at a time). This timer controls whether or not the next chord "
"that you perform deletes the preceding characters."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:381
msgid ""
"This feature allows users to misfire chords, yet be able to correct them "
"by quickly performing the chord correctly, without having to backspace "
"manually to erase the misfired chord. The result is that the timeout will"
" automatically backspace all of the preceding characters (up to the last "
"breaking character) and replace them with the intended chord."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst
msgid "Press Tolerance"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:385
msgid ""
"The press tolerance refers to a window of time in which a chord can be "
"performed, measured in milliseconds (ms). This timer is initiated upon "
"the first “press” action of the first key in a chord and ends once the "
"last key of the chord is pressed, or until the press tolerance runs out, "
"whichever comes first. Read the :ref:`GTM "
"section<GenerativeTextMenu:Press Tolerance>` for a more in-depth "
"explanation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst
msgid "Release Tolerance"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:389
msgid ""
"he release tolerance refers to a window of time in which a chord can be "
"performed, measured in milliseconds (ms). This timer is initiated upon "
"the first “release” action of any key in a chord and ends once the chord "
"is fully performed, or until the release tolerance runs out, whichever "
"comes first. Read the :ref:`GTM section<GenerativeTextMenu:Release "
"Tolerance>` for a more in-depth explanation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst
msgid "Compound Chording"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:393
msgid ""
"This toggle allows you to enable or disable :ref:`compound "
"chords<Chords:Compound Chords>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:396
msgid "RGB"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:397
msgid "The RGB settings ONLY affect the CharaChorder Lite as of February of 2024."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:399
msgid "These settings adjust the color and brightness of your CharaChorder Lite."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:-1
msgid "The RGB settings box"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:407
msgid "Library"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:-1
msgid "A picture of the Library"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:412
msgid ""
"The Library is a powerful tool that lets you add, delete and edit chords "
"stored in your chord library. It's easy to use and quick to load. We'll "
"go over how to use it below."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:414
msgid ""
"When you :ref:`connect<Device Manager:Connecting to the Device Manager>` "
"your device to the device manager, the webpage will start reading the "
"chords on your device. It may take a couple of seconds — or even over a "
"minute for very large libraries — to load the first time. If you have "
":ref:`auto-reconnect<Autoreconnect>` enabled, the loading times are much "
"shorter."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:416
msgid ""
"Chords displayed here are shown in alphabetical order, using the list of "
":ref:`chord outputs<Chords:Chord Output>`. The number of chords shown on "
"the library depends on your screen size and browser zoom settings. Above "
"the chords list, you'll see the search bar which will display the number "
"of chords on your CCOS device until something is typed there."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:420
msgid ""
"You can search through your chords by searching :ref:`chord "
"outputs<Chords:Chord Output>` (the word that displays once you've "
"performed a chord). This textbox is not case sensitive, so you can type "
"in capital or lowercase letters regardless of whether or not the chord "
"has a capital letter in it. This search bar is also intuitive enough that"
" you are also able to search partial words/phrases."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:422
msgid ""
"To the right of the search bar, you'll find two numbers separated by a "
"forward slash (``/``). These numbers indicate the page number that you're"
" on out of the total number of pages that compose your chord library. "
"Using the angle brackets to the right of those numbers will allow you to "
"flip through the different pages of your chord library which is sorted in"
" alphabetical order."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:424
msgid ""
"Under the page-turning brackets, you'll see a tall box with the text "
"\"Try typing here\". You can use this text box to test your new chords as"
" you edit them in the manager."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:426
msgid ""
"Under the text box if your device supports it you can find some shortcuts"
" to help you clear your chord library, add back in the starter chords "
"that came on your device, add functional utility chords, and download a "
"text file with all of your chord outputs separated by a pipe character "
"for importing into practice tools."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:428
msgid ""
"Finally, at the bottom of the page, if you hover over the Device name "
"you'll notice that you can hold Shift and click on it to \"Sync\".  If "
"you do this, it will have the device manager read your device's chord "
"library again. This process can take a few seconds."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:431
msgid "Creating a Chord"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:432
msgid ""
"You can follow the steps below to create a new chord on the device "
"manager."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:437
msgid ""
"Find the \"New chord\" text under the :ref:`search bar<search bar>` and "
"click it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:440
msgid ""
"When the text displays \"Hold chord,\" press and hold all of the keys "
"that you want to use as your :ref:`chord input<Chords:Chord Input>`. Once"
" you have pressed all of the keys, release the keys."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:442
msgid ""
"You will now see the :ref:`chord input<Chords:Chord Input>` in the left "
"column as letters inside individual boxes. These boxed-letters will be "
"highlighted in a color (as opposed to black or white). The color depends "
"on your selected :ref:`color scheme<Device Manager:Color Scheme>`. You "
"will also notice a single, floating dot highlighted in the same color off"
" to the right of the boxed-letters."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:445
msgid ""
"You can add any number of chords at a time without defining the desired "
":ref:`chord output<Chords:Chord Output>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:448
msgid ""
"If you click :ref:`save<Device Manager:Save Button>`, before defining a "
":ref:`chord output<Chords:Chord Output>` as described in :ref:`step "
"three<Step 3>`, any chords that you've created will save to your device "
"with a blank output and will lead to strange behavior."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:452
msgid ""
"Click into the text box to the right of the :ref:`chord "
"input<Chords:Chord Input>` that you created in the previous step and type"
" your desired :ref:`chord output<Chords:Chord Output>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst Manager.rst:586
msgid "Using Action Codes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:456 Manager.rst:499
msgid ""
"As you type your :ref:`chord output<Chords:Chord Output>`, you'll notice "
"that your cursor will have a bubble with a ``+`` above it. You can click "
"this to open the :ref:`action codes menu<Device Manager:Using Action "
"Codes>` where you can search for specific action codes or browse through "
"the action codes available to assign into a :ref:`chord "
"output<Chords:Chord Output>`. Read the :ref:`action codes section<Device "
"Manager:Using Action Codes>` for information on the different kinds of "
"action codes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:458
msgid ""
"As you type, you'll notice that your text has changed color to match your"
" :ref:`color scheme<Device Manager:Color Scheme>` and that the end of "
"your text has a floating dot immediately to the right."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:460
msgid ""
"Once you are satisfied with your :ref:`output<Chords:Chord Output>`, you "
"can proceed to modify another chord or click :ref:`save<Device "
"Manager:Save Button>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:464
msgid "Deleting a Chord"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:465
msgid "You can follow the steps below to delete a chord in the device manager."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:470
msgid "Locate the chord that you would like to delete."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:472
msgid ""
"When you hover over the chord that you would like to delete, you will "
"notice a small trash icon appear in line with that chord map. Click the "
"trash icon in order to mark it for deletion."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:474
msgid ""
"When you click the trash icon, the boxed-letters in the left column will "
"have a line through them and they will turn red. You can unmark chords "
"for deletion by clicking the \"undo\" arrow next to the trash icon. The "
"chord will return to its original color and the line will disappear."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:477
msgid ""
"You can mark multiple chords for deletion at a time. Flipping through the"
" pages in your chord library will not unmark the chords that you have "
"marked for deletion."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:479
msgid ""
"Once you have marked the undesired chords for deletion and are ready to "
"delete them, click the :ref:`save button<Device Manager:Save Button>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:481
msgid "Once you click save, the marked chord maps will disappear from the list."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:485
msgid "Editing a Chord"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:486
msgid ""
"You can follow the steps below to edit an existing chord in the device "
"manager."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:491
msgid "Locate the chord that you would like to edit."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:493
msgid ""
"Click the textbox in the right column where the :ref:`chord "
"output<Chords:Chord Output>` is displayed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:495
msgid ""
"Edit the :ref:`chord output<Chords:Chord Output>` to be whatever you "
"would like. As you type, you will notice that the text changes color to "
"match your :ref:`color scheme<Device Manager:Color Scheme>` and that the "
"end of your text has a floating dot immediately to the right."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:502
msgid ""
"You can edit multiple chords before :ref:`saving<Device Manager:Save "
"Button>` your changes. Flipping through the pages in your chord library "
"will not undo the changes that you have made to your existing chords."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:504
msgid ""
"Once you are ready to :ref:`save<Device Manager:Save Button>` your "
"changes, click :ref:`save<Device Manager:Save Button>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:506
msgid ""
"Once you click :ref:`save<Device Manager:Save Button>`, the chord(s) that"
" you've modified will change color to match the rest of the list and the "
"floating dot will disappear."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:509
msgid "Share button"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:510
msgid ""
"Next to every chord, you will see a share icon. You can share individual "
"chord maps with others by pressing this button. When you do, your "
"computer's clipboard will copy a URL that you can share with anyone who "
"can then add that exact chord map to their own CharaChorder through the "
"Device Manager."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:512
msgid ""
"When you follow a chord map link, you'll be taken to the Library where "
"you'll see the new chord map ready to be :ref:`saved<Device Manager:Save "
"Button>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:516
msgid "Layout"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:517
msgid ""
"The Device Manager has a very intuitive layer editor. It's the third "
"option in the main navigation bar at the left of the page. When you go to"
" this tab, you'll see a diagram of your device, with each key filled in "
"with the corresponding :ref:`action code<Device Manager:Using Action "
"Codes>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:521
msgid "Layer Selector"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst
msgid "Explanation of Layers on CCOS Devices"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:525
msgid ""
"As of February of 2024, :doc:`CCOS<CCOS>` devices come with three (3) "
"layers that you can make use of: the base layer, called the A1 (Alpha) "
"layer, the secondary layer, referred to as A2 (Numeric), and the tertiary"
" layer, named A3 (Function)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:527
msgid ""
"In order to access layers, you need to press and hold a \"layer access\" "
"button. You MUST hold the button in order to use keys mapped to layers "
"other than the alpha layer. The alpha layer is active by default."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:530
msgid ""
"In this section, we’ll refer only to the default layouts on CCOS devices."
" If you have modified your layout to something different, then the next "
"portion might not be accurate for your device. If you have purchased your"
" device from CharaChorder, then the following is accurate to your device."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:532
msgid "**A1 Layer**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:534
msgid ""
"The A1 layer, also known as the alpha layer, is the main layer that is "
"active by default. Your device will always be in the A1 layer upon boot."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:536
msgid "**A2 Layer**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:538
msgid ""
"The A2 layer, sometimes referred to as the numeric layer, is accessible "
"with the :doc:`A2 access key<CharaChorder Keys>`. In the Device Manager, "
"this key has the name “Numeric Layer (Left)” and “Numeric Layer (Right)”,"
" one for each hand."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:540
msgid ""
"The A2 Layer is accessible by pressing and holding one layer access "
"button. Any key that is mapped to the A2 Layer can only be accessed by "
"pressing and holding the A2 Layer access key along with the target key. "
"You do not need to :doc:`chord<Chords>` the keys together; it’s only "
"required that the A2 Layer access key is pressed while the target key is "
"pressed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:542
msgid "**A3 Layer**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:544
msgid ""
"The A3 layer, sometimes referred to as the “function layer”, is "
"accessible with the :ref:`A3 access key<CharaChorder Keys>`. In the "
"Device Manager, this key is assignable by the names “Function Layer "
"(Left)” and “Function Layer (Right)”."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:546
msgid ""
"Once you've mapped the A3 layer access buttons, the A3 Layer is "
"accessible by pressing and holding either one of them. You do not have to"
" hold them both in order to access the A3 layer. Any key that is on the "
"A3 Layer can only be accessed by pressing and holding the :doc:`A3 access"
" key<CharaChorder Keys>`, along with the target key. You do not need to "
":doc:`chord<Chords>` the keys together; it’s only required that the A3 "
"layer access key is pressed while the target key is pressed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:549
msgid ""
"The following section assumes that you have already "
":ref:`connected<Device Manager:Connecting to the Device Manager>` your "
"device to the device manager."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:-1
msgid "Image of the Layer Selector bar"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:555
msgid ""
"Above the diagram of your device, you'll see a circle with the letters "
"\"ABC\" in the middle. The circle, together with the \"wings\" on either "
"side (one on the left with the numbers \"123\" inscribed and one on the "
"right with \"fx\" stylized within), make up the layer selector. You can "
"select any one of these to view the keys that are mapped to each "
"location, on each layer."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:558
msgid "Remapping"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:559
msgid ""
"On the layer editor, you can remap your layout by using :ref:`action "
"codes<Device Manager:Using Action Codes>`. Follow the instructions below "
"to remap your device one key at a time."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:562
msgid "How to Remap Your Keys"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:567
msgid ""
"Choose the :ref:`layer<Device Manager:Layer Selector>` where you want to "
"change the key."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:569
msgid ""
"Click on the key that you would like to change. This will bring up the "
":ref:`action codes menu<Device Manager:Using Action Codes>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:571
msgid ""
"Use the search feature in the :ref:`action codes menu<Device "
"Manager:Using Action Codes>` or scroll through available :ref:`action "
"codes<Device Manager:What are Action Codes>`. Once you've found the "
"desired :ref:`action code<Device Manager:Using Action Codes>`, click on "
"it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:573
msgid ""
"Once you select the :ref:`action code<Device Manager:Using Action "
"Codes>`, you will notice that the layout diagram now reflects the "
"selected :ref:`action code<Device Manager:Using Action Codes>` "
"highlighted according to your :ref:`color scheme<Device Manager:Color "
"Scheme>` and it will be accompanied by a floating dot."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:576
msgid ""
"You can edit multiple keys before :ref:`saving<Device Manager:Save "
"Button>` your changes. Flipping through the :ref:`layers<Device "
"Manager:Layer Selector>` will not undo the changes that you have made to "
"the layout so far."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:578
msgid ""
"Once you have changed the desired key(s), click the :ref:`save "
"button<Device Manager:Save Button>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:581
msgid ""
"Your changes will not take effect until you click :ref:`save<Device "
"Manager:Save Button>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:583
msgid ""
"Once you click :ref:`save<Device Manager:Save Button>`, the highlighted "
"key(s) will lose their highlight and the floating dot will disappear. "
"Your layout diagram will be black and white."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:587
msgid ""
"You can use action codes in chord outputs as well as while "
":ref:`remapping<Device Manager:Remapping>` keys."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:590
msgid "What are Action Codes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:591
msgid ""
"Action codes are data that :doc:`CCOS<CCOS>` interprets as characters. "
"**Put simply, they are the characters that we see while typing.** These "
"include letters, numbers, special characters, function keys, and others."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:594
msgid "Action Code Menu"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:595
msgid "You can open the action codes menu one of two ways:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:597
msgid ""
"While typing a chord :ref:`chord output<Chords:Chord Output>` in the "
":ref:`library<Device Manager:Library>`, you’ll notice that your cursor "
"will have a bubble with a + above it. You can click this to open the "
"action codes menu."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:599
msgid ""
"While editing your layout in the :ref:`layout editor<Device "
"Manager:Layout>`, click on a key to bring up the action codes menu."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:601
msgid ""
"In this menu, you can scroll through :ref:`available action codes<Device "
"Manager:Available Action Codes>` by :ref:`category<Device Manager:Action "
"Code Categories>`, or simply search specific actions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:603
msgid ""
"If you ever need to leave the action codes menu, simply click the X at "
"the top right of the menu. This will close out the box and not make any "
"changes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:606
msgid "Action Code Categories"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:607
msgid ""
"There are eight different categories in the action code menu. These are: "
"ASCII Macros, ASCII, CharaChorder One, CharaChorder, CP-1252, Keyboard, "
"Mouse, and Key Codes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:636
msgid "Remove Button"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:637
msgid ""
"You can use the \"Remove\" button on the top right of the action codes "
"menu to remove the currently assigned action code from the selected key "
"in the :ref:`layout editor<Device Manager:Layout>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:639
msgid ""
"If you select the \"Remove\" button while typing a :ref:`chord "
"output<Chords:Chord Output>` in the :ref:`library<Device "
"Manager:Library>`, it will NOT remove any action. Instead, it will add a "
"\"blank\" action that will be labeled ``0x0``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:644
msgid "Available Action Codes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Device Manager.rst:645
msgid ""
"You can see the action codes below, or view them externally `here. "
"<https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1--T9bXshCIC-OVly-"
"CY3rK87fgb7AHgJl3IySh7cmHc/edit#gid=0>`__"
msgstr ""

